INT. BRIDAL SUITE, MOUNTAIN CHALET - MORNING

Chintz and antlers. A garment bag hangs on a door. Six women crowd a mirror, a hairdryer roars, and someone's phone plays a wedding march at the wrong speed.

THE BRIDE (30s, in a half-pinned dress, a veil crushed on one arm, her face fixed in perfect, polite panic) stands on a footstool. Around her, in a frieze of competence: AUNT FLORENTINA OSEI-VANCE (60s, pearls) with a threaded needle; COUSIN PRUDENCE LINDQVIST (20s, flower crown) with a glue gun; SISTER ODALYS BARTHOLOMEW (30s, matron of honor) with a clipboard; MAKEUP ARTIST JIN-HO KOWALCZYK (30s, brushes in a tool belt) with a compact; and DENISE (40s, planner, earpiece, headset) with a radio.

Everyone is speaking at once, but no one is hearing anyone.

DENISE
(into radio)
Bride is in the dress. I repeat, in the dress.

AUNT FLORENTINA
Hold still, dear, there's a gap.

COUSIN PRUDENCE
Is that glitter supposed to be on the bodice?

ODALYS
It's not glitter, it's a thread.

JIN-HO
It's glitter.

The Bride says nothing. A single tear tracks through her foundation. Jin-Ho dabs it with a sponge before it drops; Aunt Florentina stitches; Prudence mends the flower crown with the glue gun; Odalys crosses something off the clipboard; and Denise paces to the window with the radio, listening to a voice no one else can hear.

DENISE (CONT'D)
(into radio)
Repeat? The groom has what?

A pause. Denise turns, slowly. The room goes silent. The hairdryer winds down.

DENISE (CONT'D)
(to the room, brightly)
Everything's fine. Nothing to worry about.

ODALYS
Denise.

DENISE
It's fine.

ODALYS
What did he say?

DENISE
The groom would like a word.

The Bride steps off the footstool, one satin shoe at a time, and walks to the door. Aunt Florentina's needle follows her until the thread snaps. At the threshold, she stops. Takes the veil from her arm. Hands it to Denise.

THE BRIDE
(quietly)
Hold this.

She goes out. The door closes. Prudence lifts the glue gun, sets it down. Five women look at the closed door. One by one, they take out their phones.

JIN-HO
(muttering)
I knew it.
